  "grammar": {
    "explain": [
      "You know all the letters and pairs. In this last lesson on reading you learn the small marks that complete Greek spelling, and you start reading on your own.",
      {
        "h": "The accent mark (ο τόνος)"
      },
      {
        "ul": [
          "Every word of two or more syllables has one accent mark, on the stressed vowel: *καλημέρα* (ka-li-mé-ra).",
          "The stress always falls on one of the last three syllables.",
          "Words of one syllable have no accent mark: *και, το, μου*. A few take one to tell them apart from a twin: *ή* (or) and *η* (the), *πού;* (where?) and *που* (that), *πώς;* (how?) and *πως* (that).",
          "The accent can change the meaning: *πότε;* (when?) and *ποτέ* (never), *γέρος* (old man) and *γερός* (strong).",
          "On a pair, the mark goes on the second letter: *είμαι, αύριο, ουρανός*. If it is on the first one, the two letters are read separately: *τσάι* (tsá-i, tea), *ρολόι* (ro-ló-i, clock)."
        ]
      },
      {
        "h": "The dieresis (τα διαλυτικά)"
      },
      "Two dots on ι or υ also split a pair: *καΐκι* (ka-í-ki, a fishing boat), *τρόλεϊ* (tró-le-i, trolleybus). The dots and the accent can sit on the same letter: ΐ.",
      {
        "h": "Punctuation"
      },
      "The Greek question mark looks like an English semicolon: *Τι κάνεις;* (How are you?). The Greek semicolon is a raised dot (·). Commas and full stops are the same as in English.",
      {
        "h": "Capitals"
      },
      "Words written all in capitals have no accent mark: *ΚΑΛΗΜΕΡΑ, ΦΑΡΜΑΚΕΙΟ*. That is why signs are a little harder to read: you have to know where the stress goes. A capital at the start of a word keeps its accent, written just before it: *Έλα, Όχι*.",
      {
        "h": "Handwriting"
      },
      "Greeks write the small letters joined, in shapes close to the printed ones. Watch out for β, ζ, ξ, which go below the line, and for the small ν and υ, easy to read as an English v and u. Capitals are often used for forms and signs.",
      {
        "picture": "A handwritten Greek note: «Καλημέρα! Είμαι στο σπίτι.», next to the same words in print"
      },
      {
        "h": "Your first greetings"
      },
      "Read each phrase aloud, then tap it to check.",
      {
        "flash": [
          [
            "Γεια σου",
            "ya su",
            "hi, bye (to a friend)"
          ],
          [
            "Γεια σας",
            "ya sas",
            "hello, goodbye (polite or to several people)"
          ],
          [
            "Καλησπέρα",
            "kalispéra",
            "good evening"
          ],
          [
            "Καληνύχτα",
            "kaliníchta",
            "good night"
          ],
          [
            "Τι κάνεις;",
            "ti kánis?",
            "how are you?"
          ],
          [
            "Καλά",
            "kalá",
            "fine, well"
          ],
          [
            "Παρακαλώ",
            "parakaló",
            "please; you're welcome"
          ],
          [
            "Συγγνώμη",
            "sighnómi",
            "sorry, excuse me"
          ],
          [
            "Ναι",
            "ne",
            "yes"
          ],
          [
            "Όχι",
            "óchi",
            "no"
          ],
          [
            "Αντίο!",
            "adío",
            "goodbye"
          ]
        ],
        "lang": "el"
      }
    ]
  },
